---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";

/*
 * DRAFT. Do not deploy until the page has been reviewed by someone who knows
 * German data protection law. The controller is named with an email address
 * only, no postal address, by choice.
 */
const updated = "October 10, 2026";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description={`What ${site.name}'s website stores about visitors, for how long, and how to have it deleted.`}
>
  <article class="prose-page">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <div class="prose-page-content">
      <p>
        This page explains what this website stores about you when you read it
        or leave a comment, why, and for how long. Last updated {updated}.
      </p>

      <h2 id="controller">Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name}<br />
        Email: <a href="mailto:salih@mail.grokbot.com">salih@mail.grokbot.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a personal website. There are no ads, no analytics tools and
        no tracking across other websites.
      </p>

      <h2 id="hosting">Hosting and access logs</h2>
      <p>
        The site runs on Amazon Web Services (Amazon CloudFront, AWS Lambda,
        Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB). To deliver a page, AWS has to process
        your IP address and the request your browser sends. AWS processes this
        data for me as a processor under a data processing agreement. The
        servers are in the United States. AWS is certified under the EU-U.S.
        Data Privacy Framework, and its data processing agreement includes the
        EU standard contractual clauses.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each request I keep an access log entry with the date and time, the
        page address, the response status and size, how long the response
        took, the AWS edge location that served it, and the country your
        request came from. Your IP address is not written to these logs. They
        are deleted after 90 days. I use them to see which pages are read and
        to find errors.
      </p>
      <p>
        The functions behind the site also write technical logs, for example
        whether a comment was accepted or rejected and for which post. They
        contain no IP address and no cookie value, and they are deleted after
        one month.
      </p>
      <p>
        The legal basis is my legitimate interest in running the site securely
        and knowing whether it works (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      </p>

      <h2 id="cookie">The vid cookie</h2>
      <p>
        On your first visit the site sets one cookie, called <code>vid</code>.
        It holds a random ID that is not derived from your IP address or
        anything else about you. It is only sent to this site, cannot be read
        by scripts on the page, and expires after one year.
      </p>
      <p>
        I use it for two things. When I try out a new feature, I turn it on for
        some visitors before everyone, and the cookie keeps you on the same
        version of the site from one page to the next. It also makes sure the
        reader count described below counts you once per post, however often you reload.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cookie is required to show you one consistent version of the site
        (§ 25(2) no. 2 TDDDG, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). You can delete it in your
        browser at any time. You then get a new random ID on your next visit.
      </p>

      <h2 id="reader-counts">Reader counts</h2>
      <p>
        Some blog posts show how many people are reading them right now and how
        many have read them so far. For this I store a one-way hash of your
        cookie ID combined with the post's address. Your cookie ID itself is
        never stored, and you get a different hash on every post, so the
        entries for different posts cannot be linked to each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        While a post is open, your browser tells the site every 30 seconds that
        you are still reading. That entry expires 75 seconds after the last
        signal. The entry that counts you as having read a post is kept for one
        year, the same lifetime as the cookie.
      </p>

      <h2 id="comments">Comments</h2>
      <p>
        If you leave a comment, I store the name you enter (optional, shown as
        "Anonymous" if you leave it empty), your comment, the post it belongs
        to and the time you sent it. Please don't put anything in the name
        field you don't want to see in public.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every comment waits for me to read it before it appears. When a comment
        arrives I get an email with the name and the comment text, sent through
        Amazon Simple Notification Service. If I approve it, the name, the
        comment and the date are shown publicly under the post. If I don't
        approve it within 30 days, it is deleted.
      </p>
      <p>
        To stop spam, the site allows at most three comments per visitor per
        hour. For this it stores a one-way hash of your cookie ID with each
        waiting comment and in a counter that is deleted after two hours. The
        hash is removed from a comment when I approve it. Your IP address is
        not stored.
      </p>
      <p>
        The legal basis is your consent when you send the comment (Art. 6(1)(a)
        GDPR). You can withdraw it at any time. To have a comment removed, email
        me at <a href="mailto:salih@mail.grokbot.com">salih@mail.grokbot.com</a> with the post, the name you used, and roughly when
        you sent it, so I can find it. I don't keep anything that proves who
        wrote a comment, so I can't tell you which comments are yours, and I
        may ask you to quote the text.
      </p>

      <h2 id="third-parties">Content from other websites</h2>
      <p>
        Some pages load files from other websites. When that happens your
        browser connects to them directly, and they receive your IP address
        and your browser's request.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The map on the homepage is loaded from Wikimedia Commons
          (upload.wikimedia.org), run by the Wikimedia Foundation in the United
          States.
        </li>
        <li>
          Images in some older blog posts are loaded from where those posts
          were first published: Medium, DEV Community and Giphy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Talk videos are not loaded until you press play. Only then does your
        browser connect to the video provider, for YouTube through
        youtube-nocookie.com.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask me what data I hold about you, and have it corrected,
        deleted or restricted. You can object to processing based on my
        legitimate interest, ask for your data in a portable format, and
        withdraw a consent you gave. Email me at <a href="mailto:salih@mail.grokbot.com">salih@mail.grokbot.com</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also complain to a data protection authority. Mine is the
        Berliner Beauftragte für Datenschutz und Informationsfreiheit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
